PG.notes("l032", "<p class=\"summary\">Explains what CSS (Cascading Style Sheets) is, why it was invented, and the core principle behind it: keep content in HTML and appearance in a separate stylesheet language. Shows the history from styling-by-HTML-tags to CSS, and a demo site that toggles CSS on and off without changing the HTML.</p>\n<h3>What the name means</h3>\n<ul><li>CSS = Cascading Style Sheets.</li><li>'Cascading': styles are applied in layers, from the most general rules down to the most specific ones, like water dropping level by level down a waterfall. Later lessons show this in action (which rule wins when several target the same element).</li><li>'Style sheet': a language whose only job is to describe how a page should look. Compare HTML, where 'ML' means markup language (structure and content); the 'SS' in CSS stands for the style sheet part.</li><li>CSS is one of several stylesheet languages. Others include Sass (Syntactically Awesome Style Sheets) and Less (Leaner CSS). (Extra) Sass and Less are preprocessors: you write in them and they compile down to plain CSS, because browsers only understand CSS.</li><li>CSS is the one every web developer must know, and it is the focus of this section.</li></ul>\n<h3>The problem before CSS</h3>\n<ul><li>Early web pages were HTML only, so authors had almost no control over appearance. Sites looked alike and plain.</li><li>Marc Andreessen (Mosaic browser, later Netscape, later the a16z VC fund) wrote a now well-known email admitting that there was no easy way for authors to control a document's look the way a word processor allows.</li><li>The W3C's response in HTML 3.2 (1997) was to add presentation into HTML itself: the &lt;font&gt; element with color, face (typeface) and size attributes, and the &lt;center&gt; element for layout.</li><li>All of these are now deprecated, meaning they still exist in old pages but should no longer be used.</li><li>Why they were dropped: HTML is meant to describe content. Mixing colours, fonts, alignment and table borders into the markup bloats the file, buries the structure, and on a big site makes content and styling impossible to tell apart.</li></ul>\n<h3>The CSS solution: separation of concerns</h3>\n<ul><li>A W3C recommendation led by Håkon Wium Lie (regarded as the father of CSS) introduced a way to attach styles such as font colours and spacing to HTML documents from outside the content.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Lie first proposed CSS in 1994; CSS level 1 became a W3C Recommendation in December 1996, a few weeks before HTML 3.2 was published in January 1997.</li><li>This is the breakthrough: HTML holds structure and content, CSS holds presentation.</li><li>This idea is called separation of concerns: each part of a system has one job, like separate teams building a car's headlights and its wheels. Modular design like this matters throughout programming, not just in CSS.</li></ul>\n<h3>Demo: Just Add CSS</h3>\n<ul><li>Visit appbrewery.github.io/just-add-css. It starts as a bare HTML page.</li><li>A toggle button switches the stylesheet on, and the page gains new fonts, colours and layout.</li><li>Right-click and choose View Page Source in both states: the HTML is identical. Only the CSS changes.</li><li>The script tags and button logic in the page source use JavaScript, which comes later in the course; ignore them for now.</li><li>Takeaway: CSS can completely transform a site's look without touching a single line of its HTML.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">The old, deprecated way (HTML 3.2 presentational tags). Do not use.</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;center&gt;\n  &lt;font face=&quot;Arial&quot; size=&quot;5&quot; color=&quot;red&quot;&gt;Welcome to my site&lt;/font&gt;\n&lt;/center&gt;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">The modern way: plain HTML for content, CSS for the look</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;!-- HTML: content only --&gt;\n&lt;h1 class=&quot;welcome&quot;&gt;Welcome to my site&lt;/h1&gt;\n\n&lt;!-- CSS (in a stylesheet): appearance only --&gt;\n&lt;style&gt;\n  .welcome {\n    font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\n    color: red;\n    text-align: center;\n  }\n&lt;/style&gt;</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Using deprecated presentational HTML (&lt;font&gt;, &lt;center&gt;, color/face/size attributes) found in old tutorials. Use CSS instead.</li><li>Thinking CSS changes the HTML. It only changes how the same HTML is displayed.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("s05-adding-css", "<p class=\"summary\">Covers the three ways to attach CSS to HTML: inline (style attribute), internal (&lt;style&gt; element) and external (separate .css file connected with &lt;link&gt;). Explains the anatomy of a CSS rule and when each method fits, then an exercise builds a four-page site that uses all three methods.</p>\n<h3>The three methods at a glance</h3>\n<ul><li>Inline: CSS written in a style attribute on one element. Affects only that element.</li><li>Internal: CSS written inside a &lt;style&gt; element in the HTML file. Affects only that one HTML page.</li><li>External: CSS written in a separate file with a .css extension (often style.css, styles.css or main.css), connected to each page with a &lt;link&gt; element. Affects every page that links to it.</li><li>Rule of thumb from the lecture: inline for a single element, internal for a single page, external for a multi-page site (and for most real work). External is the method used for the rest of the course; the other two are still worth recognising because they show up in other people's code.</li></ul>\n<h3>Inline CSS: the style attribute</h3>\n<ul><li>style is a global attribute: it can go on any element (html, img, br, h1, anything).</li><li>It follows the usual attribute pattern: name, equals sign, then a value in quotes. The value is the CSS.</li><li>Inside the value, a declaration has two parts: the property to change and the value to set it to, separated by a colon and ended with a semicolon. Example: style=&quot;background: blue;&quot; on the &lt;html&gt; opening tag makes the whole page background blue.</li><li>Inline CSS always goes in the opening tag of the element being styled.</li><li>Downside: to give many elements the same look you must repeat the attribute on every one, which is tedious and hard to maintain. Use it sparingly: for one-off tweaks, a single element, or quick testing.</li></ul>\n<h3>Internal CSS: the &lt;style&gt; element</h3>\n<ul><li>Open a &lt;style&gt; tag and close it with &lt;/style&gt;; everything between them is CSS.</li><li>Because a &lt;style&gt; block can affect any element in the document, each rule needs a selector to say which elements it targets. Inline CSS doesn't need one because it already sits on the target element.</li><li>Rule anatomy: selector, then an opening curly brace, then one or more property: value; declarations, then a closing curly brace. It is usually spread over several lines for readability, but it is the same as writing it on one line: html { background: red; }</li><li>One &lt;style&gt; block can hold as many rules as needed, targeting as many different elements as needed.</li><li>Convention: put the &lt;style&gt; element inside &lt;head&gt;. The browser will still apply it if it's placed elsewhere, but developers keep it in the head.</li><li>Limitation: the rules only apply to the page that contains them, so internal CSS doesn't suit multi-page sites.</li></ul>\n<h3>External CSS: a .css file plus &lt;link&gt;</h3>\n<ul><li>The CSS lives in its own file. That file contains only rules (selectors, properties, values), with no HTML tags and no &lt;style&gt; wrapper.</li><li>Connect it from inside the HTML page's &lt;head&gt; with a &lt;link&gt; element. &lt;link&gt; is a void (self-closing) element: no closing tag.</li><li>rel (relationship) says what role the linked file plays for this page. For CSS it is rel=&quot;stylesheet&quot;.</li><li>href says where the file is. ./style.css means 'style.css in the same folder as this HTML file'.</li><li>Every page that links the same file gets the same styles, so one edit restyles the whole site. This is why external CSS is the standard approach.</li></ul>\n<h3>Revision used in the exercise: links and relative paths</h3>\n<ul><li>Links between pages are anchor elements: &lt;a href=&quot;./inline.html&quot;&gt;Inline&lt;/a&gt;.</li><li>./ means 'start from the current folder', then the filename follows the forward slash.</li><li>The Solution folder holds a second copy of every file. Preview solution.html to see the target, then collapse that folder in VS Code so the duplicate filenames don't get mixed up.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Inline: style attribute in the opening tag (here on &lt;html&gt; to colour the whole page)</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;html style=&quot;background: blue;&quot;&gt;\n  ...\n&lt;/html&gt;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Internal: a &lt;style&gt; block in the head with a selector and curly braces</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;head&gt;\n  &lt;style&gt;\n    html {\n      background: red;\n    }\n  &lt;/style&gt;\n&lt;/head&gt;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">External: link the stylesheet in the head; the .css file holds only rules</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;!-- index.html --&gt;\n&lt;head&gt;\n  &lt;link rel=&quot;stylesheet&quot; href=&quot;./styles.css&quot; /&gt;\n&lt;/head&gt;\n\n/* styles.css */\nhtml {\n  background: green;\n}</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Anatomy of a CSS rule</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">/* selector  { property: value; } */\nh1 {\n  color: green;\n}</pre>\n<h2>The task: 5.1 Adding CSS: three methods, four pages</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> A project folder with five files: index.html (an h1 'Three Methods of Adding CSS' and a comment asking for three links), inline.html, internal.html and external.html (each with an h1 telling you what colour and method to use), and an empty style.css. A Solution folder holds the finished versions.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Turn index.html into a home page that links to the three other pages, then colour each page's h1 using the method that page is named after: blue with inline, red with internal, green with external.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>In index.html, under the h1, add three anchor tags whose href values are ./inline.html, ./internal.html and ./external.html, with link text Inline, Internal, External.</li><li>In inline.html, add style=&quot;color: blue&quot; to the opening &lt;h1&gt; tag.</li><li>In internal.html, add a &lt;style&gt; element inside &lt;head&gt; containing a rule that selects h1 and sets color: red.</li><li>In style.css, write a rule that selects h1 and sets color: green.</li><li>In external.html, add &lt;link rel=&quot;stylesheet&quot; href=&quot;./style.css&quot; /&gt; inside &lt;head&gt; so the page loads style.css.</li><li>Preview index.html, click each link and check each h1 is the right colour. Compare with Solution/solution.html.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> A home page with the heading and three working links. The Inline page shows a blue h1 ('Style Me in Blue!'), the Internal page a red one, and the External page a green one.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;!-- index.html (body) --&gt;\n&lt;h1&gt;Three Methods of Adding CSS&lt;/h1&gt;\n&lt;a href=&quot;./inline.html&quot;&gt;Inline&lt;/a&gt;\n&lt;a href=&quot;./internal.html&quot;&gt;Internal&lt;/a&gt;\n&lt;a href=&quot;./external.html&quot;&gt;External&lt;/a&gt;\n\n&lt;!-- inline.html (body) --&gt;\n&lt;h1 style=&quot;color: blue&quot;&gt;Style Me in Blue!&lt;/h1&gt;\n\n&lt;!-- internal.html (head) --&gt;\n&lt;style&gt;\n  h1 {\n    color: red;\n  }\n&lt;/style&gt;\n\n&lt;!-- external.html (head) --&gt;\n&lt;link rel=&quot;stylesheet&quot; href=&quot;./style.css&quot; /&gt;\n\n/* style.css */\nh1 {\n  color: green;\n}</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Writing a selector and braces inside a style attribute. Inline CSS is just property: value pairs.</li><li>Forgetting the selector or curly braces inside &lt;style&gt; or a .css file.</li><li>Putting &lt;style&gt; tags inside the .css file. External files contain only CSS rules.</li><li>Wrong href in &lt;link&gt; (wrong filename, e.g. styles.css vs style.css, or wrong folder), so the stylesheet silently never loads.</li><li>Leaving out rel=&quot;stylesheet&quot;; the browser then doesn't treat the file as CSS.</li><li>Adding a closing &lt;/link&gt; tag. It is a void element.</li><li>Editing the copy in the Solution folder by mistake because it has the same filenames.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("s05-selectors", "<p class=\"summary\">Explains the selector part of a CSS rule and walks through the five basic selector types: element, class, id, attribute and universal. Ends with a five-TODO exercise where only style.css is edited to hit the right elements.</p>\n<h3>Anatomy of a rule</h3>\n<ul><li>A rule has two halves: the selector (outside the braces) decides which HTML is affected, and the declaration block inside the braces holds property: value pairs that say what changes.</li><li>A declaration is a property, a colon, then the value, e.g. color: blue. The lecture defers where to look up properties to the next section.</li><li>The selector is the part this lesson focuses on: it answers 'which elements does this styling apply to?'</li></ul>\n<h3>Element (type) selector</h3>\n<ul><li>Written as the bare tag name: h2 { color: red; }.</li><li>It matches every element of that tag in the document. With three h2s, all three turn red.</li><li>A well-formed page normally has one h1, but p, a, h2, li and so on usually appear many times, so an element selector hits all of them.</li></ul>\n<h3>Class selector</h3>\n<ul><li>Written as a dot immediately followed by the class name, with no space: .red-text { color: red; }.</li><li>A class is an attribute you add to HTML (class=&quot;red-text&quot;) to group elements; the same class can sit on different tag types, e.g. an h2 and a p, and both get the rule.</li><li>The name in the CSS must match the HTML exactly, including case. .Red-text will not match class=&quot;red-text&quot;.</li><li>Classes are the flexible way to share styling across unrelated elements on bigger, multi-page sites.</li></ul>\n<h3>ID selector</h3>\n<ul><li>Written as a hash sign immediately followed by the id value: #main { ... }, matching id=&quot;main&quot;.</li><li>Difference from a class: a class may be reused on any number of elements, whereas an id value should appear on only one element per HTML file.</li><li>Use an id when you want to style one unique element, which an element selector would over-match.</li></ul>\n<h3>Attribute selector</h3>\n<ul><li>Syntax: element, then square brackets with the attribute inside, e.g. p[draggable] { ... } selects every p that has a draggable attribute, whatever its value.</li><li>Add an equals sign and a value to match precisely: p[draggable=&quot;false&quot;] selects only paragraphs whose draggable attribute is false. With three paragraphs set to true, false, false, the first rule matches one and this rule matches two.</li><li>Works with any attribute (id, class, draggable, src, href, alt, value...), so it selects on something different from tag, class or id.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Quote the value in brackets: [value=&quot;4&quot;] is the safe, conventional form.</li></ul>\n<h3>Universal selector</h3>\n<ul><li>A single asterisk: * { ... } matches every element the stylesheet applies to, regardless of tag, class, id or attributes.</li><li>Simplest selector to understand; typically used for page-wide defaults.</li></ul>\n<h3>Specificity teaser</h3>\n<ul><li>In the exercise, the li with the green id stays green even after a li[value] rule colours all list items blue. The reason is CSS specificity (some selectors outrank others), promised for the advanced CSS section later in the course.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Rough order, weakest to strongest: universal, element, class/attribute, id, inline style.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">The five basic selectors</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">/* element: every h2 */\nh2 { color: red; }\n\n/* class: anything with class=&quot;red-text&quot; */\n.red-text { color: red; }\n\n/* id: the one element with id=&quot;main&quot; */\n#main { color: red; }\n\n/* attribute: every p that has draggable=&quot;false&quot; */\np[draggable=&quot;false&quot;] { color: red; }\n\n/* universal: everything */\n* { color: red; }</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">HTML that those selectors act on</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;h2 class=&quot;red-text&quot; id=&quot;main&quot;&gt;Heading&lt;/h2&gt;\n&lt;p class=&quot;red-text&quot; draggable=&quot;true&quot;&gt;Paragraph&lt;/p&gt;\n&lt;p draggable=&quot;false&quot;&gt;Another paragraph&lt;/p&gt;</pre>\n<h2>The task: 5.2 CSS Selectors: five TODOs</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> index.html already links style.css via a link tag and contains an h1, h2, one p (class note) and an ordered list whose li items carry class note, value attributes (2 to 5) and one id (id-selector-demo). style.css has an existing ol rule (margins, list-style-position) that must stay unchanged. The preview starts as plain black unstyled text; goal.png shows the finished look.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Complete five TODOs in order by writing only selectors in style.css, matching goal.png: centred text, a red paragraph, larger note text, one green item and one blue item.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Do not edit index.html (no changing classes, ids or values) and do not touch the existing ol rule.</li><li>TODO 1: select all paragraph tags with an element selector and set color: red.</li><li>TODO 2: select everything with class note using the class selector and set font-size: 20px. It affects the p and all li items but not the headings.</li><li>TODO 3: select the element with id id-selector-demo using the id selector and set color: green. That is list item 3.</li><li>TODO 4: select li elements whose value attribute is 4 using li[value=&quot;4&quot;] and set color: blue.</li><li>TODO 5: select all elements with * and set text-align: center.</li><li>Tip: copy class/id names from the HTML and paste them to avoid typos.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Everything centred; the paragraph red; paragraph and list items at 20px; item 3 green; item 4 blue.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">/* existing ol rule stays above */\n\np {\n  color: red;\n}\n\n.note {\n  font-size: 20px;\n}\n\n#id-selector-demo {\n  color: green;\n}\n\nli[value=&quot;4&quot;] {\n  color: blue;\n}\n\n* {\n  text-align: center;\n}</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Misspelling or mis-casing a class or id in CSS so it silently matches nothing.</li><li>Putting a space between the dot or hash and the name (. note or # id).</li><li>Reusing one id on several elements; use a class for groups.</li><li>Using only li[value] (no value) and wondering why every item is selected: the bracket selector without a value matches any element that merely has the attribute.</li><li>Expecting a later colour rule to override the green id item: specificity keeps the id rule winning.</li><li>The reference repo's TODO 5 comment mentions font-family but its code uses text-align: center, which is what the lecture asks for. It also adds a personal footer line to solution.html that should be ignored.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("s05-color-vocab", "<p class=\"summary\">Section project: style a starter HTML page that pairs colour words with colour images, writing a brand-new external stylesheet. It consolidates id, class and element selectors, plus linking a CSS file via a relative path.</p>\n<h3>Project idea</h3>\n<ul><li>A mini flashcard site for learning colour words in a foreign language (the lecture uses Spanish, but any language works). Each word is an h2 shown in the colour it names, followed by an image of that colour.</li><li>Starter index.html is supplied with the content already written. All the learner's work goes into CSS; the HTML must not be edited.</li></ul>\n<h3>Create and link the stylesheet</h3>\n<ul><li>index.html already contains a link to ./style.css but the file does not exist. Create style.css in the same folder as index.html (not inside the solution folder, not elsewhere), since the relative path in href is what the browser follows.</li><li>The file name just has to match the href exactly; style.css, styles.css or main.css are all fine if the link agrees.</li><li>Quick link test: add a rule that sets the background colour of everything (or body) to red. If the preview turns red, the link works; then delete the test rule.</li></ul>\n<h3>Style rules needed</h3>\n<ul><li>Each colour heading has a unique id (red, blue, orange, green, yellow), so an id selector like #red { color: red; } gives every title the colour it means. Repeat for all five.</li><li>Titles should have a lighter weight than default (default h2 is bold): font-weight: normal. A plain h2 selector is wrong because the page has another h2 (the subtitle) that should stay bold.</li><li>The five titles all carry class color-title, which the subtitle h2 lacks, so .color-title { font-weight: normal; } hits exactly the right group.</li><li>Images must be 200px by 200px squares: use the element selector img { height: 200px; width: 200px; }. Do this in CSS, not in HTML attributes.</li><li>Choosing selectors here is the lesson: id for a unique one-off, class for a group sharing a trait, element for all of one tag.</li></ul>\n<h3>How to work</h3>\n<ul><li>Two modes: follow the numbered TODO comments step by step, or study goal.png and build it freely.</li><li>Compare with the solution page by previewing solution.html after your attempt.</li><li>Afterwards, personalise: change colours, swap in another language, restyle further.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Complete style.css for the project</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">img {\n  height: 200px;\n  width: 200px;\n}\n\n.color-title {\n  font-weight: normal;\n}\n\n#red {\n  color: red;\n}\n\n#blue {\n  color: blue;\n}\n\n#orange {\n  color: orange;\n}\n\n#green {\n  color: green;\n}\n\n#yellow {\n  color: yellow;\n}</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">HTML structure being styled (unchanged by the learner)</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;link rel=&quot;stylesheet&quot; href=&quot;./style.css&quot; /&gt;\n...\n&lt;h1&gt;Colors&lt;/h1&gt;\n&lt;h2&gt;Learn colors in English!&lt;/h2&gt;\n&lt;h2 class=&quot;color-title&quot; id=&quot;red&quot;&gt;Red&lt;/h2&gt;\n&lt;img src=&quot;./assets/images/red.png&quot; alt=&quot;red&quot; /&gt;</pre>\n<h2>The task: 5.3 Colour Vocab Project</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Folder with index.html (links ./style.css, which is missing), assets/images with red, blue, orange, green and yellow PNGs of mixed sizes, goal.png, and a solution folder.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Create style.css so titles use their own colours with lighter weight, and all images are 200px squares, matching goal.png.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Create a new file named style.css in the same folder as index.html (matching the link's href).</li><li>Verify the link by temporarily setting a red background, previewing, then removing it.</li><li>For each of the five ids, set color to the colour the heading names.</li><li>Select .color-title and set font-weight: normal so the subtitle h2 stays bold.</li><li>Select img and set height: 200px and width: 200px.</li><li>Do not change index.html.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Five lighter-weight colour titles, each in its own colour, above five equal 200px square images; the h1 and the subtitle h2 keep default styling.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">img {\n  height: 200px;\n  width: 200px;\n}\n\n.color-title {\n  font-weight: normal;\n}\n\n#red { color: red; }\n#blue { color: blue; }\n#orange { color: orange; }\n#green { color: green; }\n#yellow { color: yellow; }</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Putting style.css in the wrong folder (or inside solution/) so the link 404s and nothing is styled.</li><li>A file name that differs from the href, including case.</li><li>Using an h2 selector for font-weight, which also unbolds the subtitle.</li><li>Editing width/height in the HTML instead of CSS.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Yellow text on a white background is hard to read; consider a darker shade such as gold or goldenrod in your own version.</li><li>The reference repo's index.html uses English words and an author footer ('Made with love'), unlike the Spanish words shown in the lecture; ignore the footer.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l036", "<p class=\"summary\">A short productivity tip: remove distractions while studying, because even brief interruptions cost a lot of focus time.</p>\n<h3>Key takeaways</h3>\n<ul><li>Brief interruptions, even a minute long, can take far longer to recover from; the lecture cites research suggesting up to roughly 25 minutes to regain prior focus.</li><li>Practical habit: before a study session, put the phone in airplane mode and out of sight, such as in a drawer.</li><li>Distractions differ per person: they may be people nearby, in which case close a door, or the time of day, in which case study early morning or late evening.</li><li>Experiment with removing them and observe whether focus and output improve.</li></ul>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Keeping the phone within reach with notifications on during coding sessions.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l037", "<p class=\"summary\">Admin article inviting learners to join the course's student community. No technical content.</p>\n<h3>Key takeaway</h3>\n<ul><li>Optional community-join page with a link to meet other students; nothing to code or revise.</li></ul>");
